=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — PC3 in Office B being completely isolated from the rest of the network — is not supported by any evidence found in the live lab. A thorough inspection of every relevant layer shows the network to be fully operational.

At the endpoint level, PC3 (10.3.0.100/24) has a correctly configured IP address and default gateway (10.3.0.1). Active ping tests from PC3 achieved zero packet loss to srv1 (10.5.0.4, same Office B), pc2 and pc1 (10.1.0.x, Office A), and laptop1 (10.6.0.100), with TTLs consistent with the expected hop counts through the routing hierarchy.

At the switching layer, aswb2 (the access switch directly serving PC3) has all bridge ports active: eth0 (to PC3) and eth1 (to dswb1) are in STP forwarding state, while eth2 (to dswb2) is in STP blocking — this is the expected redundant-uplink behaviour and represents no fault. VLAN membership (VLANs 10, 20, 30, 99) is correctly configured across all trunk ports on dswb1 and dswb2.

At the routing layer, OSPF is fully converged on dswb1 and csw1. dswb1 carries 10.3.0.0/24 as a directly connected prefix and has learned all other office subnets via dual ECMP paths through csw1 and csw2. No routes are missing or in a rejected/queued state.

Root cause: There is no fault. The user's perception of isolation appears to be a false premise — possibly due to a transient glitch that self-resolved before the ticket was investigated, or a misidentification of the affected device.

Corrective fix: No network change is required. The on-call engineer should close the ticket as "no fault found" and follow up with the user to confirm their connectivity is restored, or to collect more specific failure evidence if symptoms recur.
=== END ===
